\title{Does DeiT provide better ImageNet-1K top-1 accuracy than the original Vision Transformer under comparable training conditions? Compare matched model sizes and image resolutions, distinguish ImageNet-only training from external-data pretraining, and separate distilled from non-distilled models. Identify which comparisons support improved data efficiency and which cannot isolate the effect of architecture.}
\author{Research draft -- human review required}
\date{\today}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We propose a controlled study to determine whether the DeiT architecture yields higher ImageNet-1K top-1 accuracy than the original Vision Transformer (ViT) when model size (\textasciitilde{}86 M parameters), input resolution (224×224), training data scope (ImageNet-1K only), and distillation status are held constant. By isolating the architectural contribution from the token-based distillation introduced in DeiT, the experiment will clarify which performance gains stem from the transformer design itself and which arise from the distillation procedure. The study builds on the empirical findings reported in the DeiT paper (S1) and the original ViT work (S2).
\end{abstract}
\section{Related work}
The original Vision Transformer (ViT) achieves strong results when pre-trained on massive external corpora such as JFT-300M or ImageNet-21k, but its ImageNet-only accuracy remains modest (S2). \cite{arXiv201011929}

DeiT introduces a token-specific distillation strategy that enables competitive ImageNet performance using only ImageNet-1K data, reporting up to 85.2 \% top-1 accuracy for the distilled DeiT-B variant while training on a single GPU in under three days (S1). \cite{arXiv201212877}

Existing side-by-side numbers in the literature often differ in resolution, evaluation metric (e.g., ImageNet-Real vs ImageNet-V2), or distillation status, which prevents a direct attribution of any observed accuracy gap to the architecture alone (see the comparison pairs listed in the source material). \cite{arXiv201212877}
\section{Evidence assessment}
Several reported comparisons are incompatible because they measure different distribution shifts (ImageNet-Real vs ImageNet-V2) or use different input resolutions, so they cannot be used to claim superiority of either model under the requested ImageNet-1K top-1 metric. \cite{arXiv201212877}

Other comparisons lack a verified ImageNet-1K top-1 value for one of the models or mix resolutions (e.g., 160×160 vs 224×224), rendering the evidence insufficient to determine whether DeiT outperforms ViT when all factors are held constant. \cite{arXiv201212877}
